this is a production of Cornell  University  hi this is Frank Rossi for the Cornell  turfgrass video log this week we're  talking about mowing now in the  springtime you get that surge of growth  because we know about sixty to seventy  percent of all the clippings produced  for the entire season are produced in  the first six weeks of the growing  season so it's very typical then we're  going to mow more in the spring now for  those of you striving for a more  sustainable approach to your lawn maybe  you're going to cut back on mowing a  little bit and so what happens is you're  left to have to cut the grass when it's  really high now of course we don't want  clippings clumping up everywhere is that  can be a problem from a light shading of  the turf so it might be worthwhile to  just sort of scratch scratch them around  set the mower as high as you can try to  mow slowly so that you're distributing  the clippings and most importantly make  sure you're mowing when it's dry that  will allow for the clippings to disperse  and in the end you may find if you just  skip one mowing per month you're cutting  your mowing 25% for the entire year and  that can have a real benefit on fuel  expenses as well as improving the carbon  emissions in the environment thanks for  joining me for the Cornell turfgrass  video log this has been a production of  Cornell University on the web at  cornell.edu
 

